WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.886
Now we will teach R to remember things.

2
00:00:03.886 --> 00:00:09.287
In this lesson, you will learn to save values in variables with the assignment

3
00:00:09.287 --> 00:00:15.074
arrow, find them in the Environment pane, and group related values together in a vector.

4
00:00:15.074 --> 00:00:18.160
Let's go gently, one line at a time.

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.453
I add a note: assigning a variable.

6
00:00:22.453 --> 00:00:24.806
For this, we use the assignment operator.

7
00:00:24.806 --> 00:00:26.487
It is a small arrow:

8
00:00:26.487 --> 00:00:28.840
a less than sign, and a dash.

9
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:32.356
First line:

10
00:00:32.356 --> 00:00:35.757
name, arrow, and a name in quotes. Run it.

11
00:00:35.757 --> 00:00:38.781
Look at the Environment pane on the right.

12
00:00:38.781 --> 00:00:41.048
A new variable, name, has appeared.

13
00:00:41.048 --> 00:00:42.560
R remembers it now.

14
00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.030
Next, age, arrow, thirty.

15
00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:47.100
And location, arrow, USA.

16
00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:51.546
Oh, look at the console:

17
00:00:51.546 --> 00:00:53.102
object USA not found.

18
00:00:53.102 --> 00:00:55.048
I forgot the quotation marks.

19
00:00:55.048 --> 00:00:58.550
Without quotes, R looks for a variable called USA.

20
00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:03.220
So I add the quotes, run it again, and now it works.

21
00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:14.335
To see a value, just type the variable name and run it.

22
00:01:14.335 --> 00:01:18.280
Name gives the name. Age gives thirty. Location gives USA.

23
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:41.826
Now, a new idea: the vector.

24
00:01:41.826 --> 00:01:45.166
A vector keeps related values together in one place.

25
00:01:45.166 --> 00:01:48.505
We make it with the letter c and brackets.

26
00:01:48.505 --> 00:01:50.360
The c stands for combine.

27
00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.582
I write:

28
00:02:18.582 --> 00:02:25.460
demographic, arrow, c, and inside the brackets, name, age, and location, separated by commas.

29
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.551
Run it, and in the Environment pane you see demographic, with three values.

30
00:02:54.551 --> 00:02:58.740
R even tells us it is character, because it holds text.

31
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.027
One thing to notice:

32
00:03:11.027 --> 00:03:12.811
thirty now appears in quotes.

33
00:03:12.811 --> 00:03:18.876
A vector keeps one type, so R turned the number into text to match. Good to remember!

34
00:03:18.876 --> 00:03:20.660
You are doing really well.

